   "transcript": "[Speaker 1]\nAfter what seemed like forever, they called me in and checked me over, took my blood pressure… that kind of thing. Then I almost fainted when the nurse did the blood test! But it was over so quickly that I felt really foolish for getting so worked up about it in the first place.\n[Speaker 2]\nI mean, what a nightmare! It was raining and the bus was delayed and I ended up running the last 200 meters in the pouring rain with no umbrella. The good thing was that they were running a few minutes late too. I’d been so distracted in trying to get there on time that I hadn’t had a chance to feel nervous about the actual test, and I ended up passing with flying colours!\n[Speaker 3]\nI’d heard about stage-fright, and getting worried about your performance, but I didn’t realise it could happen at something like this. Once they fired the starting gun and we set off, I realised that I could use all that stress to my advantage and I was faster than I’ve ever been in training sessions.\n[Speaker 4]\nI got held up because I’d left a bottle of water in my hand luggage. I tried to explain, but I was so worked up and I was making such a fuss that they decided to do a full search of my bag. In the end, the plane left without me and I had to pay a fortune for a hotel that night and another flight the next day!\n[Speaker 5]\nI was singing at the top of my voice to a song on the radio, when we were hit by another motorist at the traffic lights. He’d tried to get through before they changed to red. I didn’t really understand what had happened and I think I went into shock, because it was only when the ambulance came that I realised I’d broken my leg!",
