    "These days, we could assume there is nothing really left to invent, and yet new inventions are being patented all over the world on a daily basis. Some of these inventions seem so absurd that they beg the question, “Did the inventor actually set out to create what they did, or were these things discovered by accident?” Believe it or not, many historical inventions were stumbled upon rather than actually invented.",
    "Take the humble Post-it®, for example. Those little coloured notes which stick to most surfaces and can be easily peeled off without leaving any marks or sticky residue. Well, their invention actually came about by accident rather than by design. In fact, scientist Dr Spencer Silver was experimenting with different combinations to create an adhesive which was stronger than those already on the market, when he came across the discovery which led to the Post-it®. Far from being a tougher glue than the others, this product only stuck lightly to surfaces, but allowed the user to reposition the object without causing damage. At the time, Silver couldn’t envisage a practical use for the product but continued to talk to his colleagues about his discovery, claiming that sooner or later its value would become apparent.",
