    "Another ground-breaking but accidental discovery, which has had a lasting impact on modern cuisine, is the microwave oven, an integral part of many modern kitchens. The microwave oven uses electromagnetic radiation to produce thermal energy in food, allowing it to heat more evenly than many other methods of cooking. This discovery was made by chance by an American engineer called Percy Spencer. Spencer was testing the heating effect of a high-powered microwave beam for the U.S. military when he noticed that the candy bar in his pocket was beginning to melt. Intrigued by this seemingly odd side-effect of the experiment he was conducting, he decided to place an egg next to the microwave beam to study the effect. It exploded! The following day he brought corn kernels and ‘exploded’ them to make popcorn for his colleagues, much to their delight. At the time, of course, there was no research information available about the possible side-effects of cooking food with electromagnetic radiation, although that wasn’t a major worry for Spencer. He was far more interested in developing his discovery into something that would solve a problem for the American household – being able to defrost or heat food effectively in as little time as possible. And so, the modern-day microwave takes its place in the accidental discovery hall of fame!"
